/* ===================== WEEK 2 ===================== */
C({ id: 'action-strategy', week: 2, title: 'Actions vs strategies', pri: 'Medium',
  src: { notes: '✓ §2.1', text: '✓ Ch.3 §1', lec: '✓ W2_L1a' }, prereq: ['normal-form'],
  misShort: 'Thinking a strategy is just the move you make, rather than a complete contingent plan.',
  intu: R`<p>An <b>action</b> is a single move (“bid 2”, “turn left”). A <b>strategy</b> is a complete plan: what you would do at <em>every</em> point where you might have to move, including points that are never reached. In a one-shot simultaneous game the two coincide; in sequential games they differ, which is why a tree with few moves can have many strategies.</p>`,
  why: R`<ul><li>Counting strategies in a tree is a PYQ in every paper (answers 4 and 2 for the A/B–C/D–E/F tree).</li><li>Nash equilibrium is defined over strategies, so you must list them correctly before building the matrix of a sequential game.</li></ul>`,
  formal: R`${M.def(R`<p>A <b>pure strategy</b> of player $i$ is a function assigning an available action to each of $i$’s information sets. If $i$ has information sets $h_1,\dots,h_k$ with $|A(h_j)|$ actions each, then $|S_i|=\prod_{j=1}^k|A(h_j)|$. A <b>mixed strategy</b> is a probability distribution over pure strategies.</p>`)}`,
  math: R`<p><b>Counting rule.</b> Multiply the number of actions over <em>all</em> of a player’s information sets, including ones that a strategy’s own earlier move makes unreachable. PYQ tree: player 1 moves at the root (A/B) and at the node after D (E/F) → $2\times2=4$ strategies: AE, AF, BE, BF. Player 2 has one information set (C/D) → 2 strategies.</p>`,
  ex: [
    { lvl: 'Introductory', t: 'Simultaneous game', q: R`<p>In Rock–Paper–Scissors, how many strategies does each player have?</p>`, s: R`<p>One information set with 3 actions → 3 strategies; actions and strategies coincide.</p>` },
    { lvl: 'Standard', t: 'Follower’s strategies', q: R`<p>Player 1 chooses L or R; player 2 observes and chooses a or b after either. How many strategies does player 2 have?</p>`, s: R`<p>Two information sets (after L, after R), 2 actions each: $2\times2=4$: (a,a), (a,b), (b,a), (b,b) — “what I do after L, what I do after R”.</p>` },
    { lvl: 'Exam-level', t: 'Why AE and AF are different', q: R`<p>In the PYQ tree, A ends the game. Why are AE and AF counted as different strategies?</p>`, s: R`<p>A strategy must specify a move at every information set of the player, even ones its own first move makes unreachable. AE and AF give the same outcome against everything, yet they are distinct plans. This matters: (AE,D) and (AF,D) are both Nash equilibria, but only (AF,D) is subgame perfect.</p>` }],
  conn: R`<p>Used in <a href="#/c/ef-strategy">extensive-form strategies</a> and <a href="#/c/ef-to-nf">conversion to normal form</a>.</p>`,
  mis: R`<ul><li>Dropping moves at nodes the plan itself makes unreachable (giving 3 strategies instead of 4).</li><li>Confusing a mixed strategy with a sequence of moves.</li></ul>`,
  exam: R`${M.exam(R`<p>“How many pure strategies does player 1 have?” → 4 in all five supplied papers.</p>`)}` });

C({ id: 'dse', week: 2, title: 'Dominant-strategy equilibrium (strict & weak)', pri: 'High',
  src: { notes: '✓ §2.2', text: '✓ Ch.4 §3', lec: '✓ W2_L1b' }, prereq: ['dominance'],
  misShort: 'Assuming every game has a dominant-strategy equilibrium, or that a weakly dominant one is unique.',
  intu: R`<p>If every player has a dominant strategy, the prediction is immediate: each plays it, whatever they expect of others. This is the most demanding solution concept, so few games have one — but when one exists it is very convincing.</p>`,
  why: R`<ul><li>The PD outcome is a strictly dominant strategy equilibrium (SDSE).</li><li>Truthful bidding in a second-price auction is a weakly dominant strategy equilibrium (WDSE): the basis of mechanism design.</li><li>True/false questions on DSE vs NE appear in course assignments.</li></ul>`,
  formal: R`${M.def(R`<p>A profile $a^*$ is a <b>strictly (weakly) dominant strategy equilibrium</b> if, for every player $i$, $a_i^*$ is a strictly (weakly) dominant strategy.</p>`)}`,
  math: R`<p><b>Properties.</b> (i) An SDSE is unique if it exists (each strictly dominant action is unique). (ii) Every DSE is a Nash equilibrium: $a_i^*$ is a best response to <em>every</em> $a_{-i}$, in particular to $a_{-i}^*$. (iii) The converse fails: in BoS, (B,B) is an NE but no one has a dominant strategy. (iv) A WDSE need not be unique if “weakly dominant” is read as “at least as good as every other action against everything”. With that reading, two payoff-equivalent actions that both beat the rest are each weakly dominant, giving two WDSE. With the stricter reading, which needs a strict gain over every other action somewhere, such ties rule both out.</p>`,
  ex: [
    { lvl: 'Introductory', t: 'PD', q: R`<p>Find the DSE of the Prisoner’s Dilemma.</p>`, s: R`<p>(Confess, Confess) — strictly dominant for both. It is an SDSE and hence the unique NE.</p>` },
    { lvl: 'Standard', t: 'Second-price auction', q: R`<p>Two bidders with values 10 and 7 in a sealed-bid second-price auction. What is the WDSE and its outcome?</p>`, s: R`<p>Bid your value: $(10,7)$. Bidder 1 wins and pays 7, surplus 3; bidder 2 gets 0. Bidding truthfully is weakly dominant for each.</p>` },
    { lvl: 'Exam-level', t: 'NE that is not a DSE', q: R`<p>Game: (U,L)=(2,1), (U,R)=(0,0), (D,L)=(0,0), (D,R)=(1,2). Does a DSE exist? List NE.</p>`, s: R`<p>Row: U best vs L, D best vs R → no dominant strategy; same for column. No DSE. NE: (U,L) and (D,R). So “a Nash equilibrium may or may not be a DSE” is true.</p>` }],
  conn: R`<p>Special case of <a href="#/c/nash">Nash equilibrium</a>; see <a href="#/c/sol-relations">relations among solution concepts</a>.</p>`,
  mis: R`<ul><li>DSE ⊂ NE, not the reverse.</li><li>A DSE can be Pareto-inefficient (PD).</li></ul>`,
  exam: R`${M.exam(R`<p>Course-assignment true/false: “A dominant strategy equilibrium is always a Nash equilibrium” — True; “A Nash equilibrium may or may not be a DSE” — True.</p>`)}` });

C({ id: 'iesds', week: 2, title: 'Iterated elimination of strictly dominated strategies (IESDS)', pri: 'High',
  src: { notes: '✓ §2.3', text: '✓ Ch.4 §4', lec: '✓ W2_L1c' }, prereq: ['dominance', 'assumptions'],
  misShort: 'Eliminating weakly dominated strategies while calling it IESDS, or stopping after one round.',
  intu: R`<p>A rational player never plays a strictly dominated strategy. If everyone knows that, they can delete it and look at the smaller game — where new strategies may become dominated. Keep going. Each round uses one more level of “I know that you know that I am rational”.</p>`,
  why: R`<ul><li>The auction PYQ (4 of 5 papers) asks which profile survives IESDS: <b>(2, 2)</b>.</li><li>IESDS never removes a Nash equilibrium, and if it leaves a single profile, that profile is the unique NE.</li><li>Order of elimination does not matter for strict dominance (unlike weak).</li></ul>`,
  formal: R`${M.def(R`<p>Set $A_i^0=A_i$. At round $t$, remove from each $A_i^{t-1}$ every action strictly dominated (given the others’ remaining actions $A_{-i}^{t-1}$). Stop when nothing more is removed. The survivors are the result. A game is <b>dominance-solvable</b> if exactly one profile survives.</p>`)}`,
  math: R`<p><b>Key facts.</b> (1) The surviving set is the same in any order of elimination (for strict dominance in finite games). (2) No Nash equilibrium is ever eliminated: an NE action is a best response to something that survives. (3) If one profile survives, it is the unique NE.</p>
  <p><b>PYQ auction (v₁=3, v₂=5)</b> — matrix (u₁,u₂):</p>${M.mat({ rows: ['b₁=0', 'b₁=1', 'b₁=2'], cols: ['b₂=0', 'b₂=1', 'b₂=2'], cells: [['1.5, 2.5', '0, 4', '0, 3'], ['2, 0', '1, 2', '0, 3'], ['1, 0', '1, 0', '0.5, 1.5']] })}
  <ol><li>Player 2: $b_2=2$ gives (3, 3, 1.5) vs $b_2=0$’s (2.5, 0, 0) → delete $b_2=0$.</li><li>Now columns {1, 2}. Player 1: $b_1=0$ gives (0, 0); $b_1=1$ gives (1, 0); $b_1=2$ gives (1, 0.5). $b_1=2$ strictly beats $b_1=0$ → delete $b_1=0$.</li><li>Rows {1, 2}. Player 2: $b_2=1$ gives (2, 0); $b_2=2$ gives (3, 1.5) → delete $b_2=1$.</li><li>Only column 2. Player 1: $b_1=1$ gives 0, $b_1=2$ gives 0.5 → delete $b_1=1$.</li></ol><p>Survivor: <b>(2, 2)</b>, payoffs (0.5, 1.5).</p>`,
  ex: [
    { lvl: 'Introductory', t: 'Two rounds', q: R`<p>Rows U, D; columns L, M, R. Payoffs: U: (1,0), (1,2), (0,1); D: (0,3), (0,1), (2,0). Apply IESDS.</p>`, s: R`<p>Column: R (1, 0) vs M (2, 1) — M strictly beats R → delete R. Row: now U (1, 1) vs D (0, 0) → delete D. Column: L gives 0, M gives 2 → delete L. Survivor (U, M) with (1, 2).</p>` },
    { lvl: 'Standard', t: 'Auction with v₁=5, v₂=4 (2026 PYQ)', q: R`<p>Repeat the elimination for values 5 and 4.</p>`, s: R`${M.mat({ rows: ['b₁=0', 'b₁=1', 'b₁=2'], cols: ['b₂=0', 'b₂=1', 'b₂=2'], cells: [['2.5, 2', '0, 3', '0, 2'], ['4, 0', '2, 1.5', '0, 2'], ['3, 0', '3, 0', '1.5, 1']] })}<p>Player 1: $b_1=2$ (3, 3, 1.5) strictly beats $b_1=0$ (2.5, 0, 0) → delete. Player 2 (rows 1, 2): $b_2=0$ (0, 0), $b_2=1$ (1.5, 0), $b_2=2$ (2, 1): $b_2=2$ beats both → delete $b_2=0$ and $b_2=1$. Player 1 vs column 2: $b_1=2$ gives 1.5 > 0 → survivor (2, 2). Here player 2 is the one without a pure strictly dominated strategy initially (its rows: b₂=0 (2,0,0), b₂=1 (3,1.5,0), b₂=2 (2,2,1) — every pair has a tie or reversal).</p>` },
    { lvl: 'Exam-level', t: 'Not dominance-solvable', q: R`<p>Show that IESDS removes nothing in Battle of the Sexes, and say what that implies.</p>`, s: R`<p>BoS: (B,B)=(2,1), (S,S)=(1,2), off-diagonal (0,0). Each action is the unique best reply to something, so nothing is dominated. IESDS is silent; we need Nash equilibrium (two pure + one mixed).</p>` }],
  vis: R`<ol class="steps"><li>Delete b₂=0 (dominated by b₂=2)</li><li>Delete b₁=0 (dominated by b₁=2)</li><li>Delete b₂=1 (dominated by b₂=2)</li><li>Delete b₁=1 (dominated by b₁=2) → (2,2)</li></ol>`,
  conn: R`<p>Uses <a href="#/c/assumptions">common knowledge of rationality</a>; compare <a href="#/c/iewds">IEWDS</a>; with mixtures, see <a href="#/c/nbr">never-a-best-response</a>.</p>`,
  mis: R`<ul><li>Deleting a weakly dominated strategy in an IESDS question.</li><li>Comparing the wrong player’s payoffs.</li><li>Forgetting to re-check earlier players after each deletion.</li></ul>`,
  exam: R`${M.exam(R`<p>“Which strategies survive IESDS?” — options (0,0), (1,1), (2,2), None. Answer (2,2) in every version supplied (values 3/5 and 5/4).</p>`)}` });

C({ id: 'iewds', week: 2, title: 'Iterated elimination of weakly dominated strategies (IEWDS)', pri: 'Medium',
  src: { notes: '✓ §2.3', text: '✓ Ch.4 §4', lec: '✓ W2_L1c' }, prereq: ['iesds'],
  misShort: 'Believing IEWDS is order-independent or that it never removes Nash equilibria.',
  intu: R`<p>Deleting weakly dominated strategies feels cautious — why play something that is never better and sometimes worse? But weak dominance is fragile: the order in which you delete can change the result, and genuine Nash equilibria can be thrown away.</p>`,
  why: R`<ul><li>Course assignment: “The only action profile surviving IEWDS is a unique Nash equilibrium” — <b>False</b>.</li><li>Distinguishes it from IESDS in MCQs.</li></ul>`,
  formal: R`${M.def(R`<p>Same procedure as IESDS but deleting weakly dominated actions. A surviving profile (if unique) is always a Nash equilibrium, but (i) the result can depend on the order of deletion, and (ii) other Nash equilibria may have been deleted.</p>`)}`,
  math: R`<p><b>Order-dependence example.</b></p>${M.mat({ rows: ['T', 'M', 'B'], cols: ['L', 'R'], cells: [['1, 1', '0, 0'], ['1, 1', '2, 1'], ['0, 0', '2, 1']] })}<ul><li>Order 1: delete T (weakly dominated by M: 1=1, 2>0). Then for player 2, R (1,1) weakly dominates L (1,0) → delete L. Outcome payoff (2,1).</li><li>Order 2: delete B (weakly dominated by M: 1>0, 2=2). Then L (1,1) weakly dominates R (0,1) → delete R. Outcome payoff (1,1).</li></ul><p>Different orders, different predictions. Both (T,L)/(M,L) and (M,R)/(B,R) are NE.</p>`,
  ex: [
    { lvl: 'Introductory', t: 'Weak vs strict', q: R`<p>Row: U (2, 1), D (2, 0). Can IESDS delete D? Can IEWDS?</p>`, s: R`<p>Tie against L → no strict dominance; IESDS cannot delete D. U weakly dominates D, so IEWDS can.</p>` },
    { lvl: 'Standard', t: 'Equilibrium lost', q: R`<p>(U,L)=(1,1), (U,R)=(0,0), (D,L)=(0,0), (D,R)=(0,0). NE? What does IEWDS keep?</p>`, s: R`<p>NE: (U,L) and (D,R) (at (D,R) unilateral deviations give 0 = 0). U weakly dominates D, L weakly dominates R → IEWDS keeps only (U,L). The NE (D,R) is eliminated.</p>` },
    { lvl: 'Exam-level', t: 'Lecture 3×3 discrepancy', q: R`<p>NotebookLM’s Week-2 example claims a column C is “strictly dominated” by L, but the two tie against row D (0 = 0). What kind of dominance is it and what changes?</p>`, s: R`<p>Only weak. The elimination is IEWDS, not IESDS; the result may be order-dependent and the claim that the game is solvable by strict dominance is wrong for those numbers. (Flagged in the Sources page.)</p>` }],
  conn: R`<p>Contrast with <a href="#/c/iesds">IESDS</a>; WDSE in <a href="#/c/dse">DSE</a>; relations in <a href="#/c/sol-relations">Week 3</a>.</p>`,
  mis: R`<ul><li>“Survivor = unique NE” (false).</li><li>“Order doesn’t matter” (true only for strict).</li></ul>`,
  exam: R`${M.exam(R`<p>Conceptual true/false items (course assignment). Not directly in supplied PYQs.</p>`)}` });

C({ id: 'best-response', week: 2, title: 'Best responses & best-response functions', pri: 'High',
  src: { notes: '✓ §2.4', text: '✓ Ch.4 §2, Ch.5 §1', lec: '✓ W2_L2a' }, prereq: ['game-elements'],
  misShort: 'Writing a single number for a best response when several actions tie (a best response is a set).',
  intu: R`<p>Fix what everyone else does. Your <b>best response</b> is whatever gives you the highest payoff against that. In a matrix: for each column, the row player underlines her highest first entry; for each row, the column player underlines his highest second entry.</p>`,
  why: R`<ul><li>Nash equilibrium = everyone simultaneously best-responding.</li><li>PYQs on continuous games ask for $BR_i(\cdot)$ directly (Synergies, Bertrand: 3 papers).</li></ul>`,
  formal: R`${M.def(R`<p>$B_i(a_{-i})=\{a_i\in A_i: u_i(a_i,a_{-i})\ge u_i(a_i',a_{-i})\ \forall a_i'\in A_i\}$ — a <em>set</em> (correspondence). When it is a single point for every $a_{-i}$, write the function $b_i(a_{-i})$.</p>`)}`,
  math: R`<p><b>Continuous actions.</b> If $u_i$ is differentiable and strictly concave in $a_i$, the best response solves the first-order condition $\partial u_i/\partial a_i=0$ (check corners if the action set is bounded).</p><p><b>Synergies (PYQ):</b> $u_i=(a+e_j)e_i-e_i^2$. FOC: $a+e_j-2e_i=0\Rightarrow b_i(e_j)=\dfrac{a+e_j}{2}$. Second derivative $-2<0$ ✓. Variant with $-2e_i^2$: $a+e_j-4e_i=0\Rightarrow b_i=\dfrac{a+e_j}{4}$.</p><p><b>Bertrand with differentiated products (PYQ):</b> $q_i=10-P_i+P_j$, zero cost, $\pi_i=P_i(10-P_i+P_j)$. FOC: $10-2P_i+P_j=0\Rightarrow b_i(P_j)=5+\tfrac{P_j}{2}$.</p>`,
  ex: [
    { lvl: 'Introductory', t: 'Underline method', q: R`<p>Matrix (U,L)=(3,1), (U,R)=(0,2), (D,L)=(2,0), (D,R)=(1,3). Find all best responses.</p>`, s: R`${M.mat({ rows: ['U', 'D'], cols: ['L', 'R'], cells: [['3, 1', '0, 2'], ['2, 0', '1, 3']], br: true, ne: 'auto' })}<p>Row: vs L → U; vs R → D. Column: vs U → R; vs D → R. Only (D,R) has both underlined: the NE.</p>` },
    { lvl: 'Standard', t: 'Synergies BR (2024 T3 Q122)', q: R`<p>$v_i(e_i,e_j)=(a+e_j)e_i-e_i^2$. Find $BR_i(e_j)$.</p>`, s: R`<p>$\partial v_i/\partial e_i=a+e_j-2e_i=0\Rightarrow BR_i=(a+e_j)/2$. Increasing in $e_j$: efforts are strategic complements.</p>` },
    { lvl: 'Exam-level', t: 'Bertrand BR (2025 T3 Q93)', q: R`<p>Demand $q_i=10-P_i+P_j$, no costs. Best response?</p>`, s: R`<p>$\pi_i=10P_i-P_i^2+P_iP_j$; $\partial\pi_i/\partial P_i=10-2P_i+P_j=0\Rightarrow P_i=5+P_j/2$. Upward-sloping: prices are strategic complements.</p>` }],
  vis: R`${M.plot({ xr: [0, 16], yr: [0, 16], xlab: 'P₁', ylab: 'P₂', lines: [], segs: [[5, 0, 13, 16, 'var(--acc)'], [0, 5, 16, 13, 'var(--bad)']], pts: [{ x: 10, y: 10, lab: 'NE (10,10)' }], xt: [[5, '5'], [10, '10'], [15, '15']], yt: [[5, '5'], [10, '10'], [15, '15']], legend: [['blue: P₁ = 5 + P₂/2', 'var(--acc)'], ['red: P₂ = 5 + P₁/2', 'var(--bad)']], cap: 'Bertrand best responses cross at P = 10.' })}`,
  conn: R`<p>Defines <a href="#/c/nash">Nash equilibrium</a>; continuous use in <a href="#/c/continuous-ne">continuous NE</a> and <a href="#/c/cournot">Cournot</a>; mixed version in <a href="#/c/br-graph">best-response graphs</a>.</p>`,
  mis: R`<ul><li>Differentiating with respect to the other player’s variable.</li><li>Forgetting that a BR can be a set (ties).</li><li>Plugging symmetry ($e_i=e_j$) <em>before</em> differentiating — gives the wrong FOC.</li></ul>`,
  exam: R`${M.exam(R`<p>Synergies BR asked in 2024 T3, 2025 T2 (as $BR/a$ at $e_j=a$: answer 1) and 2026 T2 (with $-2e_i^2$). Bertrand BR in 2025 T3 and 2026 T2.</p>`)}` });

C({ id: 'nash', week: 2, title: 'Nash equilibrium (pure strategies)', pri: 'High',
  src: { notes: '✓ §2.4', text: '✓ Ch.4 §2', lec: '✓ W2_L2a–b' }, prereq: ['best-response', 'eq-intro'],
  misShort: 'Checking only one player’s incentives, or treating a cell where one player is indifferent as not an NE.',
  intu: R`<p>A Nash equilibrium is a profile where each player’s choice is a best response to the others’ choices — a set of mutually consistent expectations. If you told everyone the profile in advance, no one would want to change.</p>`,
  why: R`<ul><li>The central solution concept of the course; every paper asks for pure NE counts or identification.</li><li>Exists in mixed strategies in every finite game (Week 3), but may not exist in pure strategies (drug dealer).</li></ul>`,
  formal: R`${M.def(R`<p>$a^*$ is a (pure) <b>Nash equilibrium</b> if $u_i(a_i^*,a_{-i}^*)\ge u_i(a_i,a_{-i}^*)$ for all $a_i\in A_i$ and all $i$; equivalently $a_i^*\in B_i(a_{-i}^*)$ for all $i$. <b>Strict NE:</b> all inequalities strict for $a_i\neq a_i^*$.</p>`)}`,
  math: R`<p><b>Algorithm (finite games):</b> underline best responses of every player; cells with every entry underlined are the pure NE. <b>Continuous games:</b> solve the system $a_i=b_i(a_{-i})$ for all $i$.</p><p><b>Hawk–Dove (2024 T2 Q231)</b>, $v=10$, $k=6$, $d=4$: $(H,H)=(v/2-k,v/2-k)=(-1,-1)$, $(H,D)=(10,0)$, $(D,H)=(0,10)$, $(D,D)=(v/2-d,v/2-d)=(1,1)$.</p>${M.mat({ rows: ['H', 'D'], cols: ['H', 'D'], cells: [['−1, −1', '10, 0'], ['0, 10', '1, 1']], br: true, ne: 'auto' })}<p>Pure NE: (H,D), (D,H). Outcomes that <em>cannot</em> be pure NE: (H,H) and (D,D).</p>`,
  ex: [
    { lvl: 'Introductory', t: 'Indifference still counts', q: R`<p>(U,L)=(1,1), (U,R)=(1,0), (D,L)=(0,1), (D,R)=(2,2). Is (U,L) an NE?</p>`, s: R`<p>Row vs L: U gives 1 > 0 ✓. Column vs U: L gives 1 > 0 ✓. Yes. (D,R) is also NE. Weak inequalities are enough.</p>` },
    { lvl: 'Standard', t: '2026 T2 Q8 game', q: R`<p>(T,L)=(5,4), (T,R)=(0,3), (B,L)=(3,2), (B,R)=(1,7). Pure NE?</p>`, s: R`${M.mat({ rows: ['T', 'B'], cols: ['L', 'R'], cells: [['5, 4', '0, 3'], ['3, 2', '1, 7']], br: true, ne: 'auto' })}<p>Row: vs L → T (5>3); vs R → B (1>0). Column: vs T → L (4>3); vs B → R (7>2). NE: (T,L) and (B,R) — two pure NE.</p>` },
    { lvl: 'Exam-level', t: 'No pure NE (drug dealer, 100 trades)', q: R`<p>Police rows Street/Park, dealer columns Street/Park: (80,20), (0,100) / (10,90), (60,40). Pure NE?</p>`, s: R`${M.mat({ rows: ['Street', 'Park'], cols: ['Street', 'Park'], cells: [['80, 20', '0, 100'], ['10, 90', '60, 40']], br: true, ne: 'auto', rp: 'Police', cp: 'Dealer' })}<p>Police: vs Street → Street; vs Park → Park (match). Dealer: vs Street → Park; vs Park → Street (mismatch). No cell has both underlined → no pure NE (Q232). A unique mixed NE exists (Q233) — see <a href="#/c/indifference">indifference principle</a>.</p>` }],
  conn: R`<p>Built from <a href="#/c/best-response">best responses</a>; contains <a href="#/c/dse">DSE</a>; survives <a href="#/c/iesds">IESDS</a>; extended to <a href="#/c/mixed-strategy">mixed</a> strategies; refined by <a href="#/c/spe">SPE</a>.</p>`,
  mis: R`<ul><li>Requiring strict inequality (that is strict NE).</li><li>Rejecting a profile because a different cell gives both more (that is Pareto, not Nash).</li><li>“No pure NE ⇒ no NE” — mixed NE always exists in finite games.</li></ul>`,
  exam: R`${M.exam(R`<p>Pure-NE identification appears in every paper: Hawk–Dove (MSQ), drug dealer (none), 2×2 numeric (two or none), tree games converted to matrices (3 or 4).</p>`)}` });

C({ id: 'classic-games', week: 2, title: 'Classic 2×2 games', pri: 'High',
  src: { notes: '✓ §2.5', text: '✓ Ch.4 §5–7', lec: '✓ W2_L2b–c' }, prereq: ['nash'],
  misShort: 'Mixing up the structures (Chicken/Hawk–Dove anti-coordination vs Stag Hunt coordination).',
  intu: R`<p>A handful of 2×2 games capture most strategic tensions: <b>PD</b> (private vs collective interest), <b>BoS</b> (coordinate, but disagree where), <b>Stag Hunt</b> (coordinate on the risky good outcome or the safe one), <b>Matching Pennies</b> (pure conflict, no pure NE), <b>Chicken / Hawk–Dove</b> (anti-coordination: each wants the other to back down).</p>`,
  why: R`<ul><li>PYQs are thinly disguised versions: drug dealer = Matching Pennies structure; Hawk–Dove directly.</li><li>Knowing the structure tells you immediately how many pure NE to expect.</li></ul>`,
  formal: R`<table class="t"><tr><th>Game</th><th>Structure</th><th>Pure NE</th><th>Mixed NE</th></tr><tr><td>Prisoner’s Dilemma</td><td>Dominant D; inefficient</td><td>1 (D,D)</td><td>none extra</td></tr><tr><td>Battle of the Sexes</td><td>Coordination with conflict</td><td>2</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>Stag Hunt</td><td>Coordination; payoff vs risk dominance</td><td>2</td><td>1</td></tr><tr><td>Matching Pennies</td><td>Zero-sum, cyclic BRs</td><td>0</td><td>1 (½,½)</td></tr><tr><td>Chicken / Hawk–Dove</td><td>Anti-coordination</td><td>2 (asymmetric)</td><td>1 (symmetric)</td></tr></table>`,
  math: R`<p><b>BoS:</b> (B,B)=(2,1), (S,S)=(1,2), off-diagonal (0,0). Mixed: row plays B with $p$ making column indifferent: $1\cdot p=2(1-p)\Rightarrow p=2/3$; column plays B with $q$: $2q=1-q\Rightarrow q=1/3$.</p><p><b>Stag Hunt:</b> (S,S)=(4,4), (S,H)=(0,3), (H,S)=(3,0), (H,H)=(3,3). (S,S) is payoff-dominant, (H,H) risk-dominant. Mixed: $4p=3\Rightarrow p=3/4$ on Stag.</p><p><b>Hawk–Dove (general):</b> with $k>v/2$, mixed NE: $p_H=\dfrac{v/2+d}{k+d}$. Check with $v=10,k=6,d=4$: $(5+4)/(6+4)=0.9$.</p>`,
  ex: [
    { lvl: 'Introductory', t: 'Name the game', q: R`<p>(A,A)=(1,−1), (A,B)=(−1,1), (B,A)=(−1,1), (B,B)=(1,−1).</p>`, s: R`<p>Matching Pennies: zero-sum, no pure NE, mixed (½,½).</p>` },
    { lvl: 'Standard', t: 'Hawk–Dove derivation', q: R`<p>Derive the general mixed NE of Hawk–Dove.</p>`, s: R`<p>Opponent plays H with $p$. $U(H)=p(v/2-k)+(1-p)v$; $U(D)=p\cdot0+(1-p)(v/2-d)$. Set equal: $p(v/2-k)+v-pv=(1-p)(v/2-d)$. Expanding: $pv/2-pk+v-pv=v/2-d-pv/2+pd$ ⇒ $v/2+d=p(k+d)$ ⇒ $p=\dfrac{v/2+d}{k+d}$.</p>` },
    { lvl: 'Exam-level', t: 'Hawk–Dove MSQ (2024 T2 Q231)', q: R`<p>With $v=10$, $k=6$, $d=4$, which outcomes cannot be pure NE?</p>`, s: R`<p>(H,H) = (−1,−1): either player switches to D and gets 0 > −1. (D,D) = (1,1): either switches to H and gets 10 > 1. (H,D) and (D,H) are NE. Answer: <b>(H,H) and (D,D)</b>.</p>` }],
  conn: R`<p>Mixed equilibria in <a href="#/c/indifference">indifference principle</a>; zero-sum in <a href="#/c/zero-sum">Week 3</a>.</p>`,
  mis: R`<ul><li>Thinking Hawk–Dove has a symmetric pure NE.</li><li>In BoS, assigning the row player’s mixing probability from the row player’s own payoffs (it comes from the column player’s).</li></ul>`,
  exam: R`${M.exam(R`<p>Hawk–Dove appears as an MSQ: select <em>all</em> correct options. Missing one costs the question.</p>`)}` });

C({ id: 'ne-interpret', week: 2, title: 'Interpretations of Nash equilibrium', pri: 'Supporting',
  src: { notes: '✓ §2.6', text: '✓ Ch.4 §2', lec: '✓ W2_L3' }, prereq: ['nash'],
  misShort: 'Thinking NE requires players to compute it from scratch every time.',
  intu: R`<p>Why would play end up at a Nash equilibrium? (1) <b>Rational deduction:</b> sufficiently clever players reason their way to it (convincing when unique). (2) <b>Steady state / convention:</b> in a population that plays repeatedly, behaviour settles where no one regrets their choice — like driving on the left. (3) <b>Self-enforcing agreement:</b> non-binding pre-play talk can only settle on an NE, since any other agreement would be broken. (4) <b>Learning / evolution:</b> trial and error converges on it.</p>`,
  why: R`<ul><li>Explains how to choose among multiple equilibria (focal points, conventions).</li><li>Conceptual MCQs on what NE assumes.</li></ul>`,
  formal: R`<p>The steady-state view assumes each player has correct beliefs about others’ behaviour from experience; the deductive view needs common knowledge of rationality and a unique (or focal) equilibrium.</p>`,
  math: R`<p>No new mathematics — but note the <b>2/3-of-the-average</b> game: players pick 0–100; winner is closest to 2/3 of the average. Iterated dominance: numbers above 66.7 are dominated, then above 44.4, … → unique NE is 0. Real players choose ~20–35, showing limits of deep common-knowledge reasoning.</p>`,
  ex: [
    { lvl: 'Introductory', t: 'Convention', q: R`<p>Which interpretation explains why everyone in India drives on the left?</p>`, s: R`<p>Steady state / convention: both (Left, Left) and (Right, Right) are NE; history selects one.</p>` },
    { lvl: 'Standard', t: 'Self-enforcing agreement', q: R`<p>In the PD, players agree to Deny. Will the agreement hold without enforcement?</p>`, s: R`<p>No: (Deny, Deny) is not an NE; each gains by confessing. Only NE agreements are self-enforcing.</p>` },
    { lvl: 'Exam-level', t: 'Level-k in the 2/3 game', q: R`<p>A level-0 player picks 50 on average. What do level-1 and level-2 pick?</p>`, s: R`<p>Level 1: $\tfrac23\cdot50\approx33$. Level 2: $\tfrac23\cdot33\approx22$. Infinite levels → 0, the NE.</p>` }],
  conn: R`<p>See <a href="#/c/mixed-interpret">interpretations of mixing</a> and <a href="#/c/assumptions">common knowledge</a>.</p>`,
  mis: R`<ul><li>NE is not a recommendation for collective welfare.</li><li>Communication does not make non-equilibrium agreements stable.</li></ul>`,
  exam: R`${M.exam(R`<p>Supporting theory; possible definitional MCQ.</p>`)}` });

C({ id: 'braess', week: 2, title: 'Braess’s paradox & route choice', pri: 'High',
  src: { notes: '✓ §2.7', text: '— (not in DSR chapters used)', lec: '✓ W1_L3, W2_L4' }, prereq: ['nash'],
  misShort: 'Assuming that adding a road can only reduce (or not change) travel times.',
  intu: R`<p>Adding a road can make <em>everyone’s</em> commute longer. Each driver, choosing selfishly, moves onto the new shortcut; but that crowds the congestible links everyone shares. In the new equilibrium no one wants to switch back, yet all are worse off than before.</p>`,
  why: R`<ul><li>Braess routing appears in 2024 T3, 2025 T2 and 2025 T3 (2 marks each sub-question).</li><li>A vivid example of an inefficient Nash equilibrium (like the PD) in congestion games.</li></ul>`,
  formal: R`${M.def(R`<p>A <b>congestion game</b>: each driver picks a route; a link’s travel time depends on how many use it; a driver’s cost is the sum of link times on the route. An NE is a route assignment where no driver can reduce their own time by switching routes.</p>`)}`,
  math: R`<p><b>PYQ setup (4 drivers).</b> A→X and Y→B: $3+3n$ (6 for one car, +3 per extra). A→Y: $19+n$ (20, +1). X→B: $19.1+0.9n$ (20, +0.9).</p>${M.net({ ax: '3+3n', xb: '19.1+0.9n', ay: '19+n', yb: '3+3n' }, { cap: 'Before the new road.' })}
  <p><b>Before.</b> With 2 per route: A-X-B $=9+20.9=29.9$; A-Y-B $=21+9=30$. Check deviation: a driver moving from A-X-B to A-Y-B makes it 3 on A-Y-B: $22+12=34>29.9$; moving from A-Y-B to A-X-B: $12+21.8=33.8>30$. With 3–1 splits, the crowded route is ~$12+21.8=33.8$ vs ~$20+6=26$ on the other → someone moves. So NE: <b>2 on each route, times 29.9 and 30</b>.</p>
  ${M.net({ ax: '3+3n', xb: '19.1+0.9n', ay: '19+n', yb: '3+3n', xy: '6+n' }, { cap: 'After the X→Y road (one car 7, +1 per extra).' })}
  <p><b>After.</b> Candidate: 1 on A-X-B, 2 on A-X-Y-B, 1 on A-Y-B. Link loads: AX 3, XB 1, XY 2, YB 3, AY 1. Times: AX $=12$, XB $=20$, XY $=8$, YB $=12$, AY $=20$. Routes: A-X-B $=32$, A-X-Y-B $=12+8+12=32$, A-Y-B $=20+12=32$. Deviations: A-X-B driver → A-X-Y-B: AX stays 12, XY 3→9, YB 4→15: $36>32$; A-X-Y-B driver → A-X-B: XB 2 → 20.9: $12+20.9=32.9>32$; → A-Y-B: AX 2 (9)… route A-Y-B: AY 2 → 21, YB 12: $33>32$; A-Y-B driver → A-X-Y-B: AX 4 → 15, XY 3 → 9, YB 12 → $36$. No profitable deviation: <b>NE with everyone at 32</b>, higher than the 29.9/30 before. “36 minutes” (2024 T3 option) and the “2,1,1” split (2025 T3) are wrong.</p>
  <p><b>Lecture version.</b> 4000 drivers; links A→C: $T/100$, C→B: 45, A→D: 45, D→B: $T/100$. Before: 2000 each, time $20+45=65$. With a zero-time C→D bridge, A-C-D-B costs at most $40+0+40=80$ and is a dominant choice, so everyone takes it: 80 minutes (NotebookLM Week-1 notes use a 1-minute bridge → 81; flagged).</p>`,
  ex: [
    { lvl: 'Introductory', t: 'Lecture split', q: R`<p>4000 drivers, routes A-C-B ($T/100+45$) and A-D-B ($45+T/100$). Equilibrium split and time?</p>`, s: R`<p>Equalise: $T_1/100+45=45+T_2/100$ with $T_1+T_2=4000$ → 2000 each, 65 minutes.</p>` },
    { lvl: 'Standard', t: 'Verify the pre-road NE (2024 T3 Q120)', q: R`<p>Show “two people take each route, each person’s travel time is 29.9 or 30” is correct.</p>`, s: R`<p>Computed above: 29.9 and 30, no profitable deviation (34 and 33.8 after switching). Both statements true → option 3 (“both”). In 2025 T2 the second option says “28 minutes” — false, so the answer is option 1 only.</p>` },
    { lvl: 'Exam-level', t: 'After the road (2024 T3 Q121 / 2025 T2 Q309)', q: R`<p>Is “1 on A-X-B, 2 on A-X-Y-B, 1 on A-Y-B” an NE? What is the travel time?</p>`, s: R`<p>Yes (deviation checks above); each driver takes 32 minutes. So “each person’s travel time is 36 minutes” is false (2024 T3 answer: option 1 only), while “every person’s travel time increases compared with no new road” is true (29.9/30 → 32; 2025 T2 answer: both).</p>` }],
  conn: R`<p>An inefficient <a href="#/c/nash">NE</a> like the <a href="#/c/pd">PD</a>; illustrates <a href="#/c/eq-intro">equilibrium ≠ optimum</a>.</p>`,
  mis: R`<ul><li>Forgetting that link costs depend on the total number of users across all routes sharing that link.</li><li>Counting the first car’s time wrongly (“one car takes 6, each additional +3” ⇒ $n$ cars: $6+3(n-1)$).</li><li>Checking only one type of deviation.</li></ul>`,
  exam: R`${M.exam(R`<p>Pattern: option 1 = configuration, option 2 = travel-time claim, option 3 = both, option 4 = none. Compute times carefully; the decoy times (28, 36) are close to correct ones.</p>`)}` });

C({ id: 'hotelling', week: 2, title: 'Hotelling location game (median voter)', pri: 'Medium',
  src: { notes: '✓ §2.7', text: '✓ Ch.5 (location)', lec: '✓ W2_L4' }, prereq: ['nash'],
  misShort: 'Thinking spreading out (at 1/4 and 3/4) is the equilibrium because it minimises travel.',
  intu: R`<p>Two ice-cream sellers on a beach, customers spread evenly, each customer goes to the nearer seller. Whatever the rival’s spot, you gain customers by moving towards them. The only rest point is both in the middle — even though customers would travel less if sellers were at 1/4 and 3/4. Same logic: political parties converging to the median voter.</p>`,
  why: R`<ul><li>Shows NE can be socially inefficient without any “dilemma” framing.</li><li>Lecture application; likely conceptual MCQ.</li></ul>`,
  formal: R`${M.def(R`<p>Players choose $x_1,x_2\in[0,1]$. Consumers uniform on $[0,1]$ buy from the nearer seller (split equally if tied). Payoff: market share. If $x_1<x_2$: $u_1=\dfrac{x_1+x_2}{2}$, $u_2=1-\dfrac{x_1+x_2}{2}$.</p>`)}`,
  math: R`<p><b>Unique NE:</b> $x_1=x_2=\tfrac12$. Proof sketch: (i) If $x_1<x_2$, player 1 gains by moving right (towards $x_2$) — share $(x_1+x_2)/2$ increases in $x_1$. So no NE with distinct locations. (ii) If $x_1=x_2=x\ne\tfrac12$, each gets ½; moving slightly towards the centre captures more than ½. (iii) At $x=\tfrac12$, any move gives less than ½. Hence $(\tfrac12,\tfrac12)$. Average travel distance there: $\tfrac14$; at $(\tfrac14,\tfrac34)$ it would be $\tfrac18$.</p>`,
  ex: [
    { lvl: 'Introductory', t: 'Shares', q: R`<p>$x_1=0.2$, $x_2=0.6$. Shares?</p>`, s: R`<p>Midpoint 0.4: $u_1=0.4$, $u_2=0.6$.</p>` },
    { lvl: 'Standard', t: 'Best response', q: R`<p>If $x_2=0.6$, what is player 1’s best response?</p>`, s: R`<p>Locate just left of 0.6 (share approaches 0.6). No exact maximiser in a continuum (open-set issue); in discrete versions, the adjacent point. Hence BR pushes towards the rival.</p>` },
    { lvl: 'Exam-level', t: 'Three sellers', q: R`<p>With three sellers, is “all at ½” an NE?</p>`, s: R`<p>No: each gets 1/3; one seller moving slightly left captures almost ½ of the market (all customers to the left). It is known that no pure NE exists with three players on a line segment — the two-player result does not generalise.</p>` }],
  conn: R`<p>Another application of <a href="#/c/nash">NE</a> with continuous actions; compare <a href="#/c/cournot">Cournot</a>.</p>`,
  mis: R`<ul><li>Socially optimal locations ≠ equilibrium.</li><li>Extending the 2-player result to 3 players.</li></ul>`,
  exam: R`${M.exam(R`<p>Not in supplied PYQs; conceptual MCQ possible.</p>`)}` });

C({ id: 'cournot', week: 2, title: 'Cournot duopoly (quantity competition)', pri: 'Medium',
  src: { notes: '✓ §2.7', text: '✓ Ch.5 §1', lec: '✓ W2_L5' }, prereq: ['best-response'],
  misShort: 'Using the monopoly FOC (ignoring the rival’s quantity) or confusing Cournot with Bertrand.',
  intu: R`<p>Two firms choose how much to produce; the market price falls with total output. Each firm’s best quantity falls as the rival produces more (strategic substitutes). The equilibrium total output is larger than a monopoly’s and smaller than perfect competition’s.</p>`,
  why: R`<ul><li>The canonical continuous-action NE (Week 2 assignment; Stackelberg version in Week 4).</li><li>Template for every “find BR, solve the system” question.</li></ul>`,
  formal: R`${M.def(R`<p>Inverse demand $P=a-(q_1+q_2)$ (for $Q<a$), constant marginal cost $c<a$. Profit $\pi_i=(a-q_1-q_2-c)q_i$.</p>`)}`,
  math: R`<ol><li>FOC: $\partial\pi_i/\partial q_i=a-c-2q_i-q_j=0\Rightarrow b_i(q_j)=\dfrac{a-c-q_j}{2}$ (0 if negative).</li><li>Symmetric NE: $q=\dfrac{a-c-q}{2}\Rightarrow q^*=\dfrac{a-c}{3}$.</li><li>Total $Q^*=\tfrac23(a-c)$; price $P^*=a-\tfrac23(a-c)=\dfrac{a+2c}{3}$; profit each $\dfrac{(a-c)^2}{9}$.</li><li>Compare monopoly $Q^m=\tfrac{a-c}2$ (profit $\tfrac{(a-c)^2}{4}$) and competition $Q^c=a-c$.</li></ol>`,
  ex: [
    { lvl: 'Introductory', t: 'Numbers', q: R`<p>$a=12$, $c=0$. Cournot quantities, price, profits?</p>`, s: R`<p>$q^*=4$ each, $Q=8$, $P=4$, $\pi=16$ each.</p>` },
    { lvl: 'Standard', t: 'Cartel temptation', q: R`<p>Same market; firms agree to split monopoly output (3 each). Does firm 1 want to deviate?</p>`, s: R`<p>$b_1(3)=(12-3)/2=4.5$. Profit at (3,3): $P=6$, $\pi=18$. Deviating to 4.5: $P=4.5$, $\pi_1=20.25>18$. The cartel is a PD.</p>` },
    { lvl: 'Exam-level', t: 'Asymmetric costs', q: R`<p>$P=12-Q$, $c_1=0$, $c_2=3$. NE?</p>`, s: R`<p>$q_1=(12-q_2)/2$, $q_2=(9-q_1)/2$. Substitute: $q_1=(12-(9-q_1)/2)/2\Rightarrow 4q_1=24-9+q_1\Rightarrow q_1=5$, $q_2=2$. $P=5$, $\pi_1=25$, $\pi_2=4$.</p>` }],
  vis: R`${M.plot({ xr: [0, 12], yr: [0, 12], xlab: 'q₁', ylab: 'q₂', lines: [], segs: [[0, 6, 12, 0, 'var(--acc)'], [6, 0, 0, 12, 'var(--bad)']], pts: [{ x: 4, y: 4, lab: 'NE (4,4)' }], xt: [[4, '4'], [6, '6'], [12, '12']], yt: [[4, '4'], [6, '6'], [12, '12']], legend: [['blue: q₂ = BR⁻¹ … firm 1: q₁ = (12−q₂)/2', 'var(--acc)'], ['red: firm 2: q₂ = (12−q₁)/2', 'var(--bad)']], cap: 'Downward-sloping best responses (strategic substitutes), a=12, c=0.' })}`,
  conn: R`<p>Contrast <a href="#/c/continuous-ne">Bertrand</a> (prices, upward BRs); sequential version (Stackelberg) in <a href="#/c/ef-apps">Week 4 applications</a>.</p>`,
  mis: R`<ul><li>Setting $q_1=q_2$ before differentiating.</li><li>Forgetting the non-negativity corner in BRs.</li></ul>`,
  exam: R`${M.exam(R`<p>Not in supplied Quiz-1 PYQs, but in the Week 2/4 assignments; the same algebra solves Bertrand and Synergies PYQs.</p>`)}` });

C({ id: 'continuous-ne', week: 2, title: 'NE with continuous strategies (Bertrand, Synergies)', pri: 'High',
  src: { notes: '✓ §2.7', text: '✓ Ch.5 §1–2', lec: '✓ W2_L5' }, prereq: ['best-response', 'nash'],
  misShort: 'Stopping at the best-response function and not solving the system, or getting the equilibrium payoff wrong.',
  intu: R`<p>With a continuum of actions, you cannot underline cells. Instead you derive each player’s best-response <em>function</em> and find where they intersect: the point where each is responding optimally to the other.</p>`,
  why: R`<ul><li><b>Synergies</b> in 2024 T3, 2025 T2, 2026 T2; <b>Bertrand</b> in 2025 T3 and 2026 T2 — 10 PYQ items in total.</li></ul>`,
  formal: R`${M.def(R`<p>For differentiable, strictly concave payoffs, an interior NE solves $\partial u_i(a_i,a_{-i})/\partial a_i=0$ for all $i$ simultaneously. In symmetric games, look for a symmetric solution $a_i=a_j$ <em>after</em> deriving the BRs; check uniqueness by the slopes (if $|b'|<1$, the BRs cross once).</p>`)}`,
  math: R`<p><b>Synergies</b> $v_i=(a+e_j)e_i-e_i^2$: $b_i=(a+e_j)/2$. Solve $e=(a+e)/2\Rightarrow e^*=a$. BR slopes ½ < 1 ⇒ unique. Payoff: $(a+a)a-a^2=a^2$. $BR_i(e_j=a)=a$, so $BR_i(a)/a=1$.</p>
  <p><b>Synergies variant</b> $v_i=(a+e_j)e_i-2e_i^2$: $b_i=(a+e_j)/4$; $e=(a+e)/4\Rightarrow e^*=a/3$; unique (slope ¼).</p>
  <p><b>Bertrand</b> $q_i=10-P_i+P_j$, zero cost: $b_i=5+P_j/2$; $P=5+P/2\Rightarrow P^*=10$; $q=10$, profit $100$. The game is symmetric, has a symmetric unique NE, and $P=15$ is not the equilibrium price.</p>`,
  ex: [
    { lvl: 'Introductory', t: 'Synergies NE', q: R`<p>Solve the synergies game with $a=6$.</p>`, s: R`<p>$e_1=e_2=6$; payoff $12\cdot6-36=36=a^2$.</p>` },
    { lvl: 'Standard', t: 'Bertrand incorrect statement (2025 T3 Q94)', q: R`<p>Which is incorrect: symmetric game; symmetric NE; each chooses $P=15$; unique NE?</p>`, s: R`<p>$P^*=10$, so “each chooses $P=15$” is incorrect. The other three are true.</p>` },
    { lvl: 'Exam-level', t: 'General linear BRs', q: R`<p>$b_1=\alpha+\beta x_2$, $b_2=\alpha+\beta x_1$ with $|\beta|<1$. Find the NE.</p>`, s: R`<p>Symmetric: $x=\alpha+\beta x\Rightarrow x^*=\alpha/(1-\beta)$. Synergies: $\alpha=a/2$, $\beta=1/2$ ⇒ $a$. Variant: $\alpha=a/4$, $\beta=1/4$ ⇒ $a/3$. Bertrand: $\alpha=5$, $\beta=1/2$ ⇒ 10.</p>` }],
  vis: R`${M.plot({ xr: [0, 2], yr: [0, 2], xlab: 'e₁/a', ylab: 'e₂/a', lines: [], segs: [[0, 0.5, 2, 1.5, 'var(--acc)'], [0.5, 0, 1.5, 2, 'var(--bad)']], pts: [{ x: 1, y: 1, lab: 'NE e = a' }], xt: [[0.5, '½'], [1, '1'], [2, '2']], yt: [[0.5, '½'], [1, '1'], [2, '2']], cap: 'Synergies: BRs e = (a + e_other)/2 cross once at e = a.' })}`,
  conn: R`<p>Same machinery as <a href="#/c/cournot">Cournot</a>; uses <a href="#/c/best-response">best responses</a>.</p>`,
  mis: R`<ul><li>Synergies payoff is $a^2$ (not $2a^2$, a 2025 T2 decoy).</li><li>Bertrand equilibrium price 10 (not 15, a 2025 T3/2026 T2 decoy).</li><li>Imposing symmetry before the FOC.</li></ul>`,
  exam: R`${M.exam(R`<p>Format: (1) choose the BR function; (2) choose the correct/incorrect statement about the NE. Write down $b_i$, solve, compute payoff — 3 lines.</p>`)}` });

/* ---------------- Week 2 checkpoints ---------------- */
Q({ id: 'cp-action-strategy-1', cp: 'action-strategy', week: 2, diff: 'Easy', cat: 'Definitions', type: 'mcq', stem: R`A pure strategy in an extensive-form game is:`, opts: ['The first move a player makes', 'A complete plan specifying an action at each of the player’s information sets', 'A probability distribution over actions', 'The path of play actually observed'], ans: 1, sol: R`<p>A complete contingent plan, including unreached information sets.</p>`, why: ['Only the first move.', 'Correct.', 'That is a mixed/behavioural strategy.', 'That is the outcome path.'] });
Q({ id: 'cp-action-strategy-2', cp: 'action-strategy', week: 2, diff: 'Moderate', cat: 'Extensive-form', type: 'num', stem: R`Player 2 moves after observing player 1’s choice among 3 actions, and has 2 actions at each node. How many pure strategies does player 2 have?`, ans: 8, sol: R`<p>3 information sets × 2 actions: $2^3=8$.</p>`, misc: 'Answering 6 (adding) or 2.' });
Q({ id: 'cp-action-strategy-3', cp: 'action-strategy', week: 2, diff: 'Moderate', cat: 'Extensive-form', type: 'num', stem: R`Player 1 chooses A/B at the root and, after B and a move by player 2, chooses among E/F/G. How many pure strategies does player 1 have?`, ans: 6, sol: R`<p>$2\times3=6$ (both information sets count, even if A ends the game).</p>` });
Q({ id: 'cp-action-strategy-4', cp: 'action-strategy', week: 2, diff: 'Moderate', cat: 'Conceptual', type: 'short', stem: R`Why must a strategy specify actions at information sets that the strategy itself prevents from being reached?`, sol: R`<p>Because others’ incentives depend on what you <em>would</em> do there: the credibility of threats is judged at unreached nodes (SPE), and equilibrium checks need payoffs against every contingency. Formally a strategy is a function on all of a player’s information sets.</p>` });

Q({ id: 'cp-dse-1', cp: 'dse', week: 2, diff: 'Easy', cat: 'Definitions', type: 'mcq', stem: R`A dominant strategy equilibrium is always a Nash equilibrium.`, opts: ['True', 'False'], ans: 0, src: 'GA', sol: R`<p>A dominant strategy is a best response to everything, so in particular to the others’ equilibrium strategies.</p>`, why: ['Correct.', 'DSE ⊂ NE.'] });
Q({ id: 'cp-dse-2', cp: 'dse', week: 2, diff: 'Easy', cat: 'Definitions', type: 'mcq', stem: R`A Nash equilibrium may or may not be a dominant strategy equilibrium.`, opts: ['True', 'False'], ans: 0, src: 'GA', sol: R`<p>E.g. BoS has NE but no dominant strategies.</p>`, why: ['Correct.', 'Counter-example: BoS.'] });
Q({ id: 'cp-dse-3', cp: 'dse', week: 2, diff: 'Moderate', cat: 'Conceptual', type: 'mcq', stem: R`Any game can have multiple dominant strategy equilibria.`, opts: ['True — weakly dominant strategy equilibria need not be unique', 'False — a dominant strategy equilibrium is always unique'], ans: 0, src: 'GA', key: 'derived', keyNote: 'The scanned assignment’s marking is illegible; answer derived. Strict DSE is unique; weak DSE can be multiple.', sol: R`<p>Strictly dominant strategies are unique, so an SDSE is unique. Weak dominance is different. Let player 1 have rows U = (2, 2), M = (2, 2), D = (0, 1) against L, R, and let L be strictly dominant for player 2. U and M are each at least as good as every other row against everything, so (U, L) and (M, L) are both weakly dominant strategy equilibria. The statement is therefore True, provided “dominant strategy equilibrium” includes weak dominance, as the course uses it.</p>`, why: ['Correct with weak dominance.', 'True only for strict dominance.'] });
Q({ id: 'cp-dse-4', cp: 'dse', week: 2, diff: 'Moderate', cat: 'Payoff matrix analysis', type: 'mcq', stem: R`(U,L)=(3,2), (U,R)=(1,1), (D,L)=(2,4), (D,R)=(0,3). Which is the dominant strategy equilibrium?`, opts: ['(U,L)', '(U,R)', '(D,L)', 'None exists'], ans: 0, sol: R`<p>Row: U beats D (3>2, 1>0). Column: L beats R (2>1, 4>3). DSE (U,L).</p>`, why: ['Correct.', 'R is dominated.', 'D is dominated.', 'Both have dominant strategies.'] });

Q({ id: 'cp-iesds-1', cp: 'iesds', week: 2, diff: 'Easy', cat: 'Conceptual', type: 'mcq', stem: R`A Nash equilibrium can never be eliminated by iterated elimination of strictly dominated strategies.`, opts: ['True', 'False'], ans: 0, src: 'GA', sol: R`<p>An NE action is a best response to the others’ NE actions, which also survive; a best response to a surviving profile cannot be strictly dominated.</p>`, why: ['Correct.', 'That is possible only with weak dominance.'] });
Q({ id: 'cp-iesds-2', cp: 'iesds', week: 2, diff: 'Moderate', cat: 'Payoff matrix analysis', type: 'mcq', stem: R`Rows U, D; columns L, M, R. U: (1,0), (1,2), (0,1). D: (0,3), (0,1), (2,0). What survives IESDS?`, opts: ['(U,M)', '(D,L)', '(U,L)', 'Nothing is eliminated'], ans: 0, sol: R`<p>R dominated by M → U dominates D → M dominates L → (U,M).</p>`, why: ['Correct.', 'D is deleted in round 2.', 'L is deleted in round 3.', 'R is strictly dominated by M.'] });
Q({ id: 'cp-iesds-3', cp: 'iesds', week: 2, diff: 'Moderate', cat: 'Conceptual', type: 'mcq', stem: R`Which is true of IESDS in finite games?`, opts: ['The order of elimination can change the result', 'The result does not depend on the order of elimination', 'It always leaves a single profile', 'It may eliminate a Nash equilibrium'], ans: 1, sol: R`<p>Order-independence holds for strict dominance.</p>`, why: ['True for weak, not strict.', 'Correct.', 'BoS: nothing is removed.', 'Never.'] });
Q({ id: 'cp-iesds-4', cp: 'iesds', week: 2, diff: 'Difficult', cat: 'Payoff matrix analysis', type: 'msq', stem: R`Auction with $v_1=3$, $v_2=5$, bids {0,1,2}, coin-toss ties. Select every bid that is eventually eliminated by IESDS (pure-strategy domination).`, opts: ['$b_1=0$', '$b_1=1$', '$b_1=2$', '$b_2=0$', '$b_2=1$', '$b_2=2$'], ans: [0, 1, 3, 4],
  sol: R`<p>Order: b₂=0 (by b₂=2) → b₁=0 (by b₁=2) → b₂=1 (by b₂=2) → b₁=1 (by b₁=2). Survivors b₁=2 and b₂=2.</p>`, why: ['Eliminated in round 2.', 'Eliminated in round 4.', 'Survives.', 'Eliminated in round 1.', 'Eliminated in round 3.', 'Survives.'],
  misc: 'Stopping after the first round.' });
Q({ id: 'cp-iesds-5', cp: 'iesds', week: 2, diff: 'Difficult', cat: 'Strategic reasoning', type: 'short', stem: R`Carry out IESDS on the auction with $v_1=3$, $v_2=5$ and state, at each step, which player is deleting what and why.`, sol: R`<p>(1) P2 deletes b₂=0: b₂=2 gives (3,3,1.5) > (2.5,0,0). (2) P1 deletes b₁=0: against {1,2}, b₁=2 gives (1,0.5) > (0,0). (3) P2 deletes b₂=1: against {1,2}, b₂=2 gives (3,1.5) > (2,0). (4) P1 deletes b₁=1: against b₂=2, 0.5 > 0. Survivor (2,2), payoffs (0.5,1.5). Step 1 uses P2’s rationality; step 2 uses P1 knowing P2 is rational; etc.</p>` });

Q({ id: 'cp-iewds-1', cp: 'iewds', week: 2, diff: 'Moderate', cat: 'Conceptual', type: 'mcq', stem: R`The only action profile that survives iterated elimination of weakly dominated strategies is a unique Nash equilibrium.`, opts: ['True', 'False'], ans: 1, src: 'GA', sol: R`<p>The survivor is a Nash equilibrium, but other NE may have been eliminated, so it need not be the <em>unique</em> NE.</p>`, why: ['IEWDS can delete NE.', 'Correct.'] });
Q({ id: 'cp-iewds-2', cp: 'iewds', week: 2, diff: 'Moderate', cat: 'Payoff matrix analysis', type: 'mcq', stem: R`(U,L)=(1,1), (U,R)=(0,0), (D,L)=(0,0), (D,R)=(0,0). Which NE does IEWDS eliminate?`, opts: ['(U,L)', '(D,R)', 'Both', 'None'], ans: 1, sol: R`<p>D weakly dominated by U; R weakly dominated by L → only (U,L) remains; (D,R) was an NE.</p>`, why: ['It survives.', 'Correct.', '(U,L) survives.', '(D,R) is removed.'] });
Q({ id: 'cp-iewds-3', cp: 'iewds', week: 2, diff: 'Difficult', cat: 'Strategic reasoning', type: 'short', stem: R`Using rows T (1,1),(0,0); M (1,1),(2,1); B (0,0),(2,1) (columns L, R), show that IEWDS is order-dependent.`, sol: R`<p>Delete T first (weakly dominated by M), then L is weakly dominated by R → outcome with payoff (2,1). Delete B first (weakly dominated by M), then R is weakly dominated by L → payoff (1,1). Different orders, different results.</p>` });

Q({ id: 'cp-best-response-1', cp: 'best-response', week: 2, diff: 'Easy', cat: 'Continuous games', type: 'mcq', stem: R`$v_i=(a+e_j)e_i-e_i^2$. The best response $BR_i(e_j)$ is:`, opts: ['$(a+e_j)/2$', '$a+e_j$', '$(a-e_j)/2$', '$a/2$'], ans: 0, sol: R`<p>FOC $a+e_j-2e_i=0$.</p>`, why: ['Correct.', 'Missing the factor ½.', 'Sign error.', 'Ignores $e_j$.'] });
Q({ id: 'cp-best-response-2', cp: 'best-response', week: 2, diff: 'Moderate', cat: 'Continuous games', type: 'num', stem: R`Cournot: $P=20-q_1-q_2$, $c=2$. Firm 1’s best response when $q_2=6$?`, ans: 6, sol: R`<p>$b_1=(20-2-6)/2=6$.</p>` });
Q({ id: 'cp-best-response-3', cp: 'best-response', week: 2, diff: 'Moderate', cat: 'Payoff matrix analysis', type: 'mcq', stem: R`(U,L)=(2,1), (U,R)=(2,0), (D,L)=(1,3), (D,R)=(0,2). Player 1’s best-response set against L is:`, opts: ['{U}', '{D}', '{U, D}', '∅'], ans: 0, sol: R`<p>Against L: U gives 2, D gives 1 → {U}.</p>`, why: ['Correct.', '1 < 2.', 'No tie.', 'A finite set always has a maximiser.'] });
Q({ id: 'cp-best-response-4', cp: 'best-response', week: 2, diff: 'Moderate', cat: 'Conceptual', type: 'short', stem: R`What does an upward-sloping best response mean economically? Give one PYQ example.`, sol: R`<p>Strategic complements: when the rival raises its action, your optimal action rises too. Bertrand with differentiated goods ($P_i=5+P_j/2$) and Synergies ($e_i=(a+e_j)/2$).</p>` });

Q({ id: 'cp-nash-1', cp: 'nash', week: 2, diff: 'Easy', cat: 'Payoff matrix analysis', type: 'mcq', stem: R`(T,L)=(5,4), (T,R)=(0,3), (B,L)=(3,2), (B,R)=(1,7). The game has:`, opts: ['Two pure NE', 'A unique pure NE', 'No pure NE', 'Four pure NE'], ans: 0, sol: R`<p>(T,L) and (B,R).</p>`, why: ['Correct.', 'Missed one.', 'Both diagonal cells are NE.', 'Off-diagonal cells have profitable deviations.'] });
Q({ id: 'cp-nash-2', cp: 'nash', week: 2, diff: 'Moderate', cat: 'Payoff matrix analysis', type: 'msq', stem: R`Hawk–Dove with $v=8$, $k=5$, $d=2$. Select all pure NE.`, opts: ['(H,H)', '(H,D)', '(D,H)', '(D,D)'], ans: [1, 2], sol: R`<p>(H,H)=(−1,−1), (H,D)=(8,0), (D,D)=(2,2). (H,D),(D,H) are NE; (D,D): switch to H gives 8 > 2; (H,H): switch to D gives 0 > −1.</p>` });
Q({ id: 'cp-nash-3', cp: 'nash', week: 2, diff: 'Moderate', cat: 'Payoff matrix analysis', type: 'num', stem: R`3×3 game. Rows U, M, D; columns L, C, R. U: (0,4),(4,0),(5,3). M: (4,0),(0,4),(5,3). D: (3,5),(3,5),(6,6). How many pure NE?`, ans: 1, sol: R`<p>(D,R)=(6,6): row vs R: 6 > 5 ✓; column vs D: 6 > 5 ✓. Others cycle (U,L)→… none. One pure NE.</p>` });
Q({ id: 'cp-nash-4', cp: 'nash', week: 2, diff: 'Difficult', cat: 'Strategic reasoning', type: 'short', stem: R`Explain why the absence of a pure NE in the drug-dealer game follows from the “cycle” of best responses, without computing any mixed strategy.`, sol: R`<p>Police want to match the dealer (Street→Street, Park→Park); the dealer wants to mismatch (Street→Park, Park→Street). Starting at any cell, one player always wants to switch, producing a cycle (SS→SP→PP→PS→SS). A pure NE needs a cell where nobody switches, which a full cycle rules out.</p>` });

Q({ id: 'cp-classic-games-1', cp: 'classic-games', week: 2, diff: 'Easy', cat: 'Conceptual', type: 'mcq', stem: R`Which classic game has no pure-strategy Nash equilibrium?`, opts: ['Battle of the Sexes', 'Stag Hunt', 'Matching Pennies', 'Prisoner’s Dilemma'], ans: 2, sol: R`<p>Matching Pennies: cyclic best responses.</p>`, why: ['2 pure NE.', '2 pure NE.', 'Correct.', '1 pure NE.'] });
Q({ id: 'cp-classic-games-2', cp: 'classic-games', week: 2, diff: 'Moderate', cat: 'Mixed strategies', type: 'num', stem: R`Hawk–Dove with $v=10$, $k=6$, $d=4$. In the symmetric mixed NE, what is the probability of Hawk?`, ans: 0.9, sol: R`<p>$p=(v/2+d)/(k+d)=9/10$.</p>` });
Q({ id: 'cp-classic-games-3', cp: 'classic-games', week: 2, diff: 'Moderate', cat: 'Conceptual', type: 'mcq', stem: R`In Stag Hunt (S,S)=(4,4), (S,H)=(0,3), (H,S)=(3,0), (H,H)=(3,3), which statement is correct?`, opts: ['(H,H) is payoff-dominant', '(S,S) is risk-dominant', '(S,S) is payoff-dominant and (H,H) is risk-dominant', 'Hunting hare is strictly dominant'], ans: 2, sol: R`<p>(S,S) gives both more; Hare guarantees 3 whatever the other does — safer.</p>`, why: ['(S,S) pays more.', 'Stag is riskier.', 'Correct.', 'Against S, Stag (4) > Hare (3).'] });

Q({ id: 'cp-ne-interpret-1', cp: 'ne-interpret', week: 2, diff: 'Easy', cat: 'Conceptual', type: 'mcq', stem: R`Which agreement made in non-binding pre-play communication can be self-enforcing?`, opts: ['Any Pareto-optimal profile', 'Only a Nash equilibrium profile', 'Any profile both prefer to the status quo', 'Only dominant-strategy profiles'], ans: 1, sol: R`<p>Off-equilibrium agreements are broken by a profitable unilateral deviation.</p>`, why: ['(Deny,Deny) in PD is Pareto-optimal but not self-enforcing.', 'Correct.', 'Same problem.', 'Too narrow.'] });
Q({ id: 'cp-ne-interpret-2', cp: 'ne-interpret', week: 2, diff: 'Moderate', cat: 'Numerical calculations', type: 'num', stem: R`In the “guess 2/3 of the average” game (0–100), what is the unique Nash equilibrium guess?`, ans: 0, sol: R`<p>Iterated dominance shrinks the range to {0}.</p>` });
Q({ id: 'cp-ne-interpret-3', cp: 'ne-interpret', week: 2, diff: 'Moderate', cat: 'Conceptual', type: 'short', stem: R`Contrast the “rational deduction” and “steady state” interpretations of NE. Which is more convincing when a game has several equilibria?`, sol: R`<p>Deduction: players reason to equilibrium using common knowledge of rationality — compelling only if the NE is unique or focal. Steady state: equilibrium arises from repeated experience in a population; history selects among multiple NE (conventions). With multiple NE the steady-state view is more convincing.</p>` });

Q({ id: 'cp-braess-1', cp: 'braess', week: 2, diff: 'Moderate', cat: 'Numerical calculations', type: 'num', stem: R`PYQ network after the X→Y road: loads 1 (A-X-B), 2 (A-X-Y-B), 1 (A-Y-B). What is each driver’s travel time (minutes)?`, ans: 32, sol: R`<p>AX=12, XB=20, XY=8, YB=12, AY=20 → 32 on every route.</p>` });
Q({ id: 'cp-braess-2', cp: 'braess', week: 2, diff: 'Moderate', cat: 'Numerical calculations', type: 'num', stem: R`PYQ network before the new road, 2 drivers on A-X-B. Their travel time?`, ans: 29.9, sol: R`<p>AX with 2 cars: 9; XB with 2 cars: 20.9. Total 29.9.</p>` });
Q({ id: 'cp-braess-3', cp: 'braess', week: 2, diff: 'Difficult', cat: 'Strategic reasoning', type: 'mcq', stem: R`After the new road, is the split “2 on A-X-B, 1 on A-X-Y-B, 1 on A-Y-B” a Nash equilibrium?`, opts: ['Yes', 'No — an A-X-B driver gains by switching to A-X-Y-B', 'No — the A-Y-B driver gains by switching to A-X-Y-B', 'No — an A-X-B driver gains by switching to A-Y-B'], ans: 1,
  sol: R`<p>Loads: AX 3 (12), XB 2 (20.9), XY 1 (7), YB 2 (9), AY 1 (20). Times: A-X-B 32.9, A-X-Y-B 28, A-Y-B 29. An A-X-B driver switching to A-X-Y-B: AX 12, XY 2 → 8, YB 3 → 12: total 32 < 32.9 → profitable. Not an NE.</p>`, why: ['A profitable deviation exists.', 'Correct (32 < 32.9).', 'A-Y-B → A-X-Y-B: AX 4 → 15, XY 8, YB 9: 32 > 29, not profitable.', 'A-X-B → A-Y-B: AY 21 + YB 12 = 33 > 32.9.'] });
Q({ id: 'cp-braess-4', cp: 'braess', week: 2, diff: 'Easy', cat: 'Conceptual', type: 'mcq', stem: R`With $N$ motorists and two symmetric routes (as in lecture), in Nash equilibrium:`, opts: ['Both paths attract an equal number of motorists', 'All motorists take the same path', 'The split depends on who moves first', 'There is no equilibrium'], ans: 0, src: 'GA', key: 'derived', keyNote: 'Assignment diagram only partly legible; answer derived from the symmetric lecture network.', sol: R`<p>Symmetric congestible routes equalise at an even split (65 minutes in the lecture numbers).</p>`, why: ['Correct.', 'Then the other route would be faster.', 'Static game.', 'An equilibrium exists.'] });
Q({ id: 'cp-braess-5', cp: 'braess', week: 2, diff: 'Difficult', cat: 'Strategic reasoning', type: 'short', stem: R`Explain in words why adding a road raised everyone’s travel time from about 30 to 32 minutes.`, sol: R`<p>The new link makes A-X-Y-B attractive to individuals because it uses the two short roads while they are lightly used. As drivers shift onto it, the short narrow roads (A-X, Y-B) become congested for everyone, including those on the original routes. In the new NE everyone takes 32 minutes, but no single driver can gain by switching back — the private benefit of the shortcut ignores the congestion cost imposed on others.</p>` });

Q({ id: 'cp-hotelling-1', cp: 'hotelling', week: 2, diff: 'Easy', cat: 'Continuous games', type: 'mcq', stem: R`Two sellers on a uniform beach [0,1], customers go to the nearer one. Nash equilibrium locations:`, opts: ['(0, 1)', '(1/4, 3/4)', '(1/2, 1/2)', '(1/3, 2/3)'], ans: 2, sol: R`<p>Each moves towards the other until both sit at the median.</p>`, why: ['Each wants to move inward.', 'Social optimum, not NE.', 'Correct.', 'Each gains by moving inward.'] });
Q({ id: 'cp-hotelling-2', cp: 'hotelling', week: 2, diff: 'Moderate', cat: 'Numerical calculations', type: 'num', stem: R`Locations $x_1=0.3$, $x_2=0.7$. What market share does seller 1 get?`, ans: 0.5, sol: R`<p>Midpoint 0.5 → share 0.5.</p>` });
Q({ id: 'cp-hotelling-3', cp: 'hotelling', week: 2, diff: 'Moderate', cat: 'Conceptual', type: 'short', stem: R`Why is the Hotelling equilibrium socially inefficient?`, sol: R`<p>With both sellers at ½, average customer distance is ¼; at (¼, ¾) it would be ⅛ with the same 50–50 split. Each seller ignores customers’ travel cost and only cares about share, so the equilibrium clusters at the centre.</p>` });

Q({ id: 'cp-cournot-1', cp: 'cournot', week: 2, diff: 'Moderate', cat: 'Continuous games', type: 'num', stem: R`Cournot duopoly: $P=100-Q$, $c=10$. Equilibrium quantity of each firm?`, ans: 30, sol: R`<p>$(a-c)/3=90/3=30$.</p>` });
Q({ id: 'cp-cournot-2', cp: 'cournot', week: 2, diff: 'Moderate', cat: 'Continuous games', type: 'num', stem: R`Same market ($P=100-Q$, $c=10$). Equilibrium price?`, ans: 40, sol: R`<p>$Q=60$, $P=40$.</p>` });
Q({ id: 'cp-cournot-3', cp: 'cournot', week: 2, diff: 'Moderate', cat: 'Conceptual', type: 'mcq', stem: R`In Cournot, best-response functions are downward sloping because:`, opts: ['Quantities are strategic substitutes', 'Quantities are strategic complements', 'Firms collude', 'Marginal cost is increasing'], ans: 0, sol: R`<p>More rival output lowers your marginal revenue, so you produce less.</p>`, why: ['Correct.', 'Complements give upward BRs.', 'No collusion.', 'Cost is constant.'] });
Q({ id: 'cp-cournot-4', cp: 'cournot', week: 2, diff: 'Difficult', cat: 'Continuous games', type: 'num', stem: R`$P=12-Q$, $c_1=0$, $c_2=3$. Firm 1’s Cournot equilibrium quantity?`, ans: 5, sol: R`<p>Solve $q_1=(12-q_2)/2$, $q_2=(9-q_1)/2$ ⇒ $q_1=5$, $q_2=2$.</p>` });

Q({ id: 'cp-continuous-ne-1', cp: 'continuous-ne', week: 2, diff: 'Moderate', cat: 'Continuous games', type: 'num', stem: R`Bertrand: $q_i=10-P_i+P_j$, zero cost. Equilibrium profit of each firm?`, ans: 100, sol: R`<p>$P=10$, $q=10$, profit 100.</p>` });
Q({ id: 'cp-continuous-ne-2', cp: 'continuous-ne', week: 2, diff: 'Moderate', cat: 'Continuous games', type: 'mcq', stem: R`$v_i=(a+e_j)e_i-2e_i^2$. The Nash equilibrium is:`, opts: ['$e_1=e_2=a/3$, unique', '$e_1=e_2=a$, unique', '$e_1=e_2=a/4$', 'No NE'], ans: 0, sol: R`<p>$e=(a+e)/4\Rightarrow e=a/3$; BR slope ¼ < 1 ⇒ unique.</p>`, why: ['Correct.', 'That is the $-e_i^2$ version.', 'That is $BR(0)$.', 'An NE exists.'] });
Q({ id: 'cp-continuous-ne-3', cp: 'continuous-ne', week: 2, diff: 'Moderate', cat: 'Continuous games', type: 'num', stem: R`Synergies $v_i=(a+e_j)e_i-e_i^2$ with $a=3$. Each player’s equilibrium payoff?`, ans: 9, sol: R`<p>$e=3$; $(3+3)3-9=9=a^2$.</p>` });
Q({ id: 'cp-continuous-ne-4', cp: 'continuous-ne', week: 2, diff: 'Difficult', cat: 'Continuous games', type: 'num', stem: R`Bertrand variant: $q_i=12-2P_i+P_j$, zero cost. Symmetric equilibrium price?`, ans: 4, sol: R`<p>$\pi_i=P_i(12-2P_i+P_j)$; FOC $12-4P_i+P_j=0\Rightarrow P_i=3+P_j/4$; $P=3+P/4\Rightarrow P=4$.</p>` });
Q({ id: 'cp-dse-5', cp: 'dse', week: 2, diff: 'Moderate', cat: 'Strategic reasoning', type: 'short', stem: R`Why is bidding your true value weakly (not strictly) dominant in a sealed-bid second-price auction?`, sol: R`<p>The price you pay is the rival’s bid, so your bid only decides whether you win. Bidding $v$ wins exactly when winning is profitable (rival bid < $v$). A higher bid can make you win at a price above $v$; a lower bid can make you lose a profitable win. So truthful bidding is never worse — but against many rival bids a different bid gives the <em>same</em> result, so the dominance is only weak.</p>` });
Q({ id: 'cp-classic-games-4', cp: 'classic-games', week: 2, diff: 'Moderate', cat: 'Strategic reasoning', type: 'short', stem: R`Classify Hawk–Dove and Stag Hunt as coordination or anti-coordination games and explain what that implies for their pure equilibria.`, sol: R`<p>Stag Hunt is a coordination game: each wants to do what the other does, so the pure NE are the symmetric profiles (S,S) and (H,H). Hawk–Dove is anti-coordination: each wants to do the opposite of the other, so the pure NE are the asymmetric profiles (H,D) and (D,H); the symmetric outcome arises only in the mixed equilibrium.</p>` });
Q({ id: 'cp-cournot-5', cp: 'cournot', week: 2, diff: 'Moderate', cat: 'Strategic reasoning', type: 'short', stem: R`Why can two Cournot firms not sustain the monopoly output by agreement in a one-shot game?`, sol: R`<p>At the shared monopoly output each firm’s best response is to produce more (e.g. 4.5 > 3 when $a=12$, $c=0$), raising its own profit at the rival’s expense. Since agreements are not binding, each deviates; the only stable outcome is the Cournot NE. Structurally a PD.</p>` });
Q({ id: 'cp-continuous-ne-5', cp: 'continuous-ne', week: 2, diff: 'Moderate', cat: 'Strategic reasoning', type: 'short', stem: R`Explain why the synergies game has a unique Nash equilibrium.`, sol: R`<p>Best responses $e_i=(a+e_j)/2$ are straight lines with slope ½. Plotted on the same axes, one has slope ½ and the other slope 2, so they intersect exactly once (at $e_1=e_2=a$). More generally, linear BRs with $|\beta|<1$ define a contraction with a unique fixed point.</p>` });
